## Migration Analysis

Your code has these Thread.Abort patterns:

| Location | Pattern | Issue on .NET 8 |
|----------|---------|-----------------|
| `Stop()` → `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on abort | Never triggered (abort can't happen) |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can wake up
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we control the loop via the token.
                // If you want to continue processing remaining items, just
                // let the loop iterate; the while-condition will exit cleanly.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — cooperative, not preemptive
2. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`** — cleanup logic preserved
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removed** — the loop naturally continues or exits via `token.IsCancellationRequested`
4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(2s)`** — actually waits for the thread instead of hoping
5. **`CancellationToken` passed into `GetNextItem`/`ProcessItem`** — so blocking calls can be interrupted

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **`GetNextItem()` must accept the token** | If it blocks on I/O or a queue without checking the token, `Cancel()` won't wake it up. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne()` instead of `Thread.Sleep()`, or async I/O with the token. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and callbacks. Always `Dispose()` it. |
| **Cancellation isn't instant** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation only fires at checkpoints where your code checks the token. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not someone else's. Without it, you could mask bugs. |

If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `BlockingCollection<T>`, the fix is straightforward:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
var item = _queue.Take();            // blocks forever, only Thread.Abort wakes it

// After (.NET 8)
var item = _queue.Take(token);       // wakes immediately on cancellation
```
